Committed. Here's what happened.

## The preferred outcome landed — and the drift it was supposed to catch had already happened

I reproduced the defect first: sed | grep -cE "include_str!|CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR|evolve\.sh|read_to_string" over test_is_mechanical_commit_covers_harness_vocabulary returns 0. A loop over hand-written literals asserting a list agrees with itself, under a doc comment promising drift "shows up as a failing test."

Then I read the authority, and it was worse than the issue claimed. scripts/evolve.sh emits 13 git commit -m templates. Only 6 are covered. Two of the misses are in live history right now:

• 2d31726f Day 181 (13:56): assessment — the vocabulary carries `assessment (draft); the harness dropped the (draft)` and nothing noticed.
• `95f053c8 Day 182: update day counter` — never covered at all.

Plus fix build errors and revert session changes (could not fix build). **The exact drift the comment promised to catch already happened, and the test stayed green through it.**

## What I built

test_evolve_sh_commit_subjects_are_covered_or_registered, in the shape the repo's five other gates use. The hard part was extraction, not assertion: subjects are built with shell interpolation, so I reduce each template to its literal runs (splitting at $VAR/${...}/$(...)/$((...))) and ask whether is_mechanical_commit matches any. That's sound in exactly one direction, which is the direction I need — a literal run is a substring of every subject the template can emit.

Fatal on the unnamed case with both remedies printed verbatim; a register for named exceptions; a two-direction ratchet. The gate does not forbid an uncovered subject, it forbids an unnamed one.

## Two things I refused to smooth over

The register is not uniform debt, and saying it was would be its own dishonesty. `$task_title (Task $TASK_NUM) is *correct by design* — a delivery commit must stay non-mechanical or a real repair loses its corroborating power. $msg` is invisible by construction. Calling those "debt" would read as a to-do list where three entries are load-bearing design.

I changed no vocabulary. Fixing the four drifted subjects in the same breath would have made the register's size unreadable as a measurement — and the register's size is the finding.

Controls run serially (my own archive: two file-mutating controls in one parallel block raced once, and one falsely passed). All four fired: pasteable remedy, anti-vacuous branch, ratchet, restore-green.

Three limits print on every passing run through a raw stderr handle, because libtest eats macro output from passing tests and a gate whose limits are invisible reads as stronger than it is. The sharpest one: it proves the vocabulary still covers what the harness emits, never that the coverage is correct.

6029 → 6479 in the register, same commit. #739 died to four lines; this started 86 in.
